% Not worth citing in the book; worth remembering why.
@InProceedings{Smith2007,
  author="Smith, Michael Anthony
  and Gibbons, Jeremy",
  editor="Davies, Jim
  and Gibbons, Jeremy",
  title="Unifying Theories of Objects",
  booktitle="Integrated Formal Methods",
  year="2007",
  publisher="Springer Berlin Heidelberg",
  address="Berlin, Heidelberg",
  pages="599--618",
  abstract="We present an approach to modelling Abadi-Cardelli-style object calculi as Unifying Theories of Programming (UTP) designs. Here we provide a core object calculus with an operational small-step evaluation rule semantics, and a corresponding UTP model with a denotational relational predicate semantics. For clarity, the UTP model is defined in terms of an operand stack, which is used to store the results of subprograms. Models of a less operational nature are briefly discussed. The consistency of the UTP model is demonstrated by a structural induction proof over the operations of the core object calculus. Overall, our UTP model is intended to provide facilities for encoding both object-based and class-based languages.",
  isbn="978-3-540-73210-5",
  doi={10.1007/978-3-540-73210-5_31},
  url={https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/jeremy.gibbons/publications/uto.pdf},
  note={.
    Meh, S&G just take a calculus from A&C
    (same as their 1996 article or chapter 6 of their 1996 book),
    with their magic λ renamed to ς to cheat their type system,
    then formalize it using a formalism from Hoare and He
    called UTP (Unifying Theories of Programming),
    that involves non-deterministic choice.
    Then they refine that with a model of stack vs heap allocation of intermediate data.
    A nice exercise, but disappointing for who was attracted to the title.
  }
}

